= Ecevit Meets President Sunay

Ecevit had asked to meet with the President in private prior to Nihat Erim's resignation, and so an arrangement was made. On April 29, the designated day for their meeting, Ecevit is driven by the presidential vehicle to Çankaya Palace from his house, where he meets with Cevdet Sunay, now the President of the Republic and formerly Chief of the General Staff. Ecevit has had a tense relationship with members of the military, especially with his staunch criticism of the March 12 Memorandum and refusal to cooperate with it. Sunay has been very suspicious of Ecevit's increasingly outspoken views.

During the meeting, Ecevit attempts to clarify his positions. He explains that although he is opposed to the military becoming involved in civilian politics, he does not hold these views against the armed forces as a whole. Ecevit goes on to explain that while the army is an important part of the Republic, defending the army does not mean accepting its interference in parliamentary democracy. Sunay is also concerned by Ecevit's political views, particularly regarding communism. Sunay is concerned about how much the Left of Center movement might encourage the more radical left.

During the meeting, Sunay asks Ecevit what he intends to do should he succeed İnönü as Chairman of the CHP. As Ecevit has grown increasingly influential in the party, it is impossible to ignore his influence, even among those who are suspicious of his political ambitions. In order to reassure the President of his intentions, Ecevit, who had already started to gradually soften his stance on opposition to military ever since he realized he had a real shot at becoming the next General Chairman, offers some assurances.

However, Ecevit does not completely abandon the principles that have caused friction with the military in the first place. Whether Ecevit and Sunay changed their opinions of each other during the meeting is unclear. Neither man revealed exactly what was discussed, and so even İsmet İnönü, who is still Chairman of the CHP, has no knowledge of what passed between his former protégé and the President in private.

